\ read-line ( stream -- str | none ): one line, terminator dropped, the bytes
\ after it left in the stream. Read over stdin, the one stream WASI has without
\ fork/exec, so this runs on both platforms; the cases needing a pipe are in
\ 158_stdout_to_string.h2o. The harness sees 157_read_line.stdin beside this
\ file and so passes this program as a file argument, feeding that file on stdin.

stdin to requests

\ === lines arrive one per call, terminator dropped ===
requests read-line . cr                              \ alpha
requests read-line . cr                              \ beta

\ === an empty line is an empty string, not none ===
requests read-line dup byte-size . none? . cr        \ 0 0
requests read-line . cr                              \ x

\ === a carriage return before the terminator is content, as under "\n" split ===
requests read-line byte-size . cr                    \ 5
"crlf" [ 13 10 ] codepoints>string + "\n" split 0 @i byte-size . cr   \ 5

\ === no buffering: read answers every byte read-line did not consume ===
requests read-line . cr                              \ one
requests read . cr                                   \ two, three, each on its own line

\ === end of input is none ===
requests read-line none? . cr                        \ 1

\ === errors ===
requests close
[: requests read-line :] catch drop :message @ . cr  \ (a closed stream)
[: 3 read-line :] catch drop :message @ . cr         \ (not a stream)
